 "category": "laboratory",
 "organism_scope": "prokaryote-generic",
 "aerobic": true,
 "description": "T-R minimal medium + Guaymas Basin hydrothermally-influenced sediment (mixed labile+complex organic matter) — medium reported in PMC10942773 (complex/undefined base — extracts not BiGG-mappable).",
 "namespace": "bigg",
 "provenance": {
  "source_type": "literature",
  "citation": "Medium composition from PMC10942773 (see GrowthDB record).",
  "doi": "",
  "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC10942773/",
  "notes": "Full recipe recovered from the primary literature: base medium 'Modified Tibbles-Rawlings (T-R) minimal medium' (Tibbles BJ, Rawlings DE. Characterization of nitrogen-fixing bacteria from a temperate saltmarsh lagoon, including isolates that produce ethane from acetylene. Microbial Ecology 1994;27(1):65-80. DOI 10.1007/BF00170115. Exact modified recipe used is given verbatim in Table S3 of the study's Supporting Information (Mahmoudi et al., ISME J 2024;18(1):wrae018, DOI 10.1093/ismejo/wrae018)",
  "verification": "paper-verified (recipe recovered from primary literature)",
  "verification_evidence": "Vibrio sp. 1A01 and Pseudoalteromonas sp. 3D05 were coincubated at room temperature with ~22 g (dry weight) of decarbonated, sterilized sediment. Sediment total organic carbon (TOC) content was previously measured to be ~3.5%, equating to ~770 mg of organic carbon (final concentration of ~385 mg C L−1) ... along with ... T-R minimal medium",
  "verification_confidence": "low",
  "recipe_evidence": "Same T-R base as Table S3. Carbon amendment (main text): hydrothermally-influenced Guaymas Basin sediment, ~22 g dry weight added, ~770 mg organic carbon (mixed labile + complex organic matter), i.e. ~385 mg C L-1 in the 2 L culture. Aerobic (~20% O2).",
  "recipe_confidence": "high",
  "base_medium": "Modified Tibbles-Rawlings (T-R) minimal medium",
  "base_medium_reference": "Tibbles BJ, Rawlings DE. Characterization of nitrogen-fixing bacteria from a temperate saltmarsh lagoon, including isolates that produce ethane from acetylene. Microbial Ecology 1994;27(1):65-80. DOI 10.1007/BF00170115. Exact modified recipe used is given verbatim in Table S3 of the study's Supporting Information (Mahmoudi et al., ISME J 2024;18(1):wrae018, DOI 10.1093/ismejo/wrae018); trace-metal solution per Tibbles & Rawlings 1994 and vitamin solution per Finster et al., Arch Microbiol 1992;157:425-430 (DOI 10.1007/BF00249099).",
